SM(498,{"n":"Jude 12-25","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Jude 12-25.mp3","d":2247.9,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.96,"Verse 12, these are spots in your love feasts."],[24.48,"Well, they feast with you without fear, serving only themselves."],[28.96,"So that word spots is actually more like stains."],[32.72,"That's spot in the Lady Macbeth sense."],[37.28,"There's stains on your love feast."],[39.94,"When you're getting together to celebrate the Eucharist."],[42.6,"They're there and feasting."],[44.28,"They're there celebrating, receiving like everybody else, without a care in the world."],[49.01,"And the fact that you're just accepting them and letting this go on."],[53.03,"That's a stain on what you're doing."],[56.47,"It's dragging down what you're doing."],[58.39,"There are clouds without water carried about by the winds"],[64.25,"So, what's a cloud without water?"],[66.33,"Pretty useless. Throws up some shade, but not even shade that keeps you from getting sunburned."],[74.99,"Laid autumn trees without fruit."],[78.76,"We're past the harvest."],[80.76,"No fruit. Useless."],[83.64,"Twice dead. Pulled up by the roots."],[89.96,"Raging waves of the sea, foaming up their own shame."],[95.08,"Wandering store stars for whom is reserved the blackness of darkness forever."],[99.88,"Bad people He doesn't like them."],[102.32,"Yeah. Umteen. Now Enoch, the seventh from Adam, prophesied about these men also, saying, we'll pause here."],[119.44,"He's about to quote the book of Enoch."],[122.96,"So Saint Jude not only doesn't have a problem with quoting the book of Enoch, but is quoting it specifically as Enoch, the seventh from Adam in the genealogy, said this."],[141.3,"So is he saying that first Enoch should be part of the Old Testament?"],[145.5,"If you're Ethiopian, sure."],[147.5,"No. No, he's not saying that."],[152.08,"Remember, we saw St."],[153.2,"Paul quoted Menander at one point, who was a great Greek comic playwright."],[160.08,"He's the only one to win the Athenian festival three times, but still not part of the Old Testament, I assure you."],[166.98,"With his comedic plays."],[168.9,"But St. Paul could quote him to the church in Corinth because this was someone they knew."],[174.5,"So even St."],[175.22,"Paul made pop culture sub-reerenfces."],[178.18,"Thus I justify my naked villainy."],[181.62,"But so he's he's not talking about, again, he has no idea of canon."],[189.3,"He's quoting this text that all of them knew"],[191.96,"And as we've talked about before, the book of Enoch, sometimes now called First Enoch, was one of the most popular Jewish texts at this time."],[200.6,"If you look at the Dead Sea Scrolls, and you look at the number of scrolls."],[204.93,"Number of copies of books."],[206.93,"That's sort of how we gauge how important a text was to a community."],[210.45,"How many copies did they have?"],[213.17,"They had copies of everything that's in our Old Testament except uh Esther and Ecclesiastes."],[220.21,"But they also had some other stuff."],[221.97,"And if you look at the number of scrolls, number one was Genesis."],[225.01,"They had the most copies of Genesis."],[226.93,"Number two was the book of Enoch."],[229.73,"Number three was the Book of Jubilees."],[231.65,"Number four was Exodus."],[236.57,"And obviously, it ended up in the Ethiopic Old Testament."],[240.89,"So the Ethiopian Jews were using it."],[243.29,"We know it's going around in Egypt."],[246.41,"Because we found copies."],[247.69,"So it's all over the place."],[249.37,"So this is a story they had heard."],[251.55,"This is something they knew about."],[255.23,"And he has no problems."],[256.67,"Now, some later folks will have a problem with this."],[263.29,"And there's this interesting thing that happens in church history."],[267.37,"Interesting to me anyway, so I'm going to make you all listen to it too."],[270.77,"Um, in that in in when you look at like the second century and the early third century. A.D. the church fathers, mostly in the West, who are familiar with St"],[284.53,"Jude's Epistle. In the East, they're mostly not familiar with it."],[288.07,"It doesn't show up on their lists of like New Testament books."],[294.47,"Those Western authors who are familiar with St."],[296.87,"Jude's Epistle argue that it should be read in the churches."],[303.37,"It should be what we would call a canonical book because it quotes the book of Enoch."],[312.01,"So at that point in church history, the book of Enoch was still very popular."],[317.22,"St. Irenaeus references it all over the place."],[319.46,"St. Justin Martyr references it all over the place."],[321.82,"All through the second century's church fathers are quoting the Book of Enoch, Tertullian's super into it."],[327.98,"Um Christians are reading it, and they use that to argue to people who aren't familiar with St."],[334.62,"Jude's Epistle that they should read St."],[336.94,"Jude's Epistle. When you get to the end of the third century, or the end, yeah, the end of the third century, the beginning of the fourth century, especially in the fourth century."],[348.2,"People start trying to make the opposite argument."],[351.58,"Because now the book of Enoch is on the outs."],[354.32,"And pretty much everyone has had accepted St."],[356.32,"Jude's Epistle. So they start saying, Well, look, St."],[359.68,"Jude's Epistle that we all accept quotes the book of Enoch, so see, you should accept the book of Enoch."],[365.2,"Well, there's a little more"],[365.64,"So they make the exact opposite argument."],[368.6,"So why did Enoch go out of fashion?"],[372.92,"So the Book of Enoch, uh, most of it, really falls out of fashion as you get into the 5th century."],[380.28,"But even in the 5th century, St."],[382.28,"Augustine, who pretty much hated the Book of Enoch, comes to this thing in St."],[387.29,"Jude, and he's like, well, okay, look."],[391.85,"Uh clearly, St."],[394.57,"Jude is saying that Enoch wrote something."],[398.5,"He's like, but whatever Enoch wrote, that would have been like before the flood."],[406.2,"So how can we know that this book of Enoch we have now is the same thing that Enoch wrote."],[417.81,"So, yeah, St."],[419.57,"Jude says Enoch wrote something, but we're not sure that this is it, so you can't trust this."],[425.7,"So St. Augustine has to do all that gymnastics to get around to like ignore the book of Enoch."],[431.22,"So why didn't you find the same logic in Genesis?"],[435.94,"Um He didn't hate the book of Genesis."],[440.82,"That's a St."],[441.7,"Augustine rather liked the book of Genesis."],[443.7,"This was supposed to be written by Moses, so that's after it was."],[446.06,"Yeah Yeah. And that's after the flood."],[447.94,"But no, but but like, if you read Tertullian and stuff, they had whole stories about how Noah had a copy of the Book of Enoch on the Ark."],[456.78,"And that's how it got through the flood."],[459.1,"I don't know what that would have looked like, because I don't know what the writing system would have been, but anyway, they had a whole explanation."],[466.68,"Um and then what actually happens, so most people today would think, like, well, church fathers just dumped the book of Enoch at some point."],[477.65,"They hashed it out and dumped it."],[479.73,"But that didn't actually happened."],[483.17,"So when you get into like the ninth century, and St."],[489.33,"Nikephoros, the patriarch of Constantinople, he published a uh a list of books, scriptural books from the library at Constantinople in the 9th century."],[504.65,"And in it he has multiple categories."],[509.37,"So he has here's the books that we all accept."],[512.81,"Here's books that some Christian communities accept and not others."],[516.89,"And then here's books that we don't read in the churches, but that are good to read at home."],[523.85,"Interestingly, even in the ninth century, the book of Revelation was still in the books we disagree about in the East."],[532.17,"Um, but he puts the Book of Enoch in the books that you should read in the home"],[538.05,"That we don't read in church, but that you should you should read in the home."],[542.69,"Along with a bunch of other Jewish texts in his Old Testament category that we find getting copied in Orthodox monasteries all the way up into the like the 15th and 16th centuries."],[554.93,"Then they get the printing press, so they don't need to hand copy them anymore."],[558.27,"Um so the reality is it sort of never went away."],[563.23,"And uh George Cinkelos, who worked with St."],[565.63,"Nikephoros to write um the Byzantine chronology, which was like the history of the world up to that point, just cribbed everything before the flood from the book of Enoch."],[577.23,"Just treated it like a historical document at the end of the eighth and beginning of the ninth century, so it never really went away."],[585.07,"It's always been kind of Bible adjacent in the East"],[590.55,"People just don't know about it."],[593.59,"In uh in modern times."],[597.51,"So anyway, St."],[599.03,"Jude quotes it here."],[602.84,"And um again, he quotes it here."],[607.8,"Here's a specific quote Behold, the Lord comes with ten thousands of his saints to execute judgment on all, to convict all who are ungodly among them of all their ungodly deeds, which they have committed in an ungodly way, and of all the harsh things which ungodly sinners have spoken against him."],[626.52,"That's a direct quote."],[628.12,"Yes. Well then St."],[629.76,"Augustine should have recognized this."],[632.62,"But he's saying, okay, so sure, that one verse."],[636.86,"But there's more than a hundred chapters of the book of Enoch."],[640.86,"So. Yeah. And the Book of Enoch also is clearly a composite book."],[646.62,"Meaning there's clearly like chunks that circulated separately that were all later put together."],[653.66,"Because they use different vocabulary and stuff and record different visions and stuff."],[659.74,"So, um, yeah, I don't want to go too much into the book of Enoch."],[665.26,"Well, I do, but I'm not going to go too much into the Book of Enoch."],[668.5,"Um, this will come up again when we get into the book of Revelation."],[672.62,"Because it's not just St."],[673.74,"Jude here. We're gonna see more references in 2 Peter"],[674.66,"We saw a reference to it in 1 Peter."],[679.3,"Book of Revelation is full of them, and there's a fair number of references to the Book of Enoch in St."],[683.7,"Matthew's Gospel, also, actually."],[686.5,"Um, so this is one of those texts and sets of stories that's out there."],[692.52,"And that the readers and writers of the the New Testament and the early Christians and the early church fathers are all familiar with."],[700.48,"And it's not something for the vast majority of them that they're reading in the synagogue or in churches."],[706.88,"There are exceptions to that, both Jewish communities like Qumran, where the Dead Sea Scrolls are from, and Ethiopia."],[714.48,"There are exceptions, but most of them are not reading it in formal worship, but they're reading it at home."],[717.52,"worship. They're reading it outside of"],[726.12,"But you can also see how this is attached to St."],[729.64,"Jude's theme. That the Lord is coming, 10,000 of his holy ones."],[736.03,"That's what saints means."],[736.99,"It's talking about angels."],[738.05,"This is talking about, as we heard uh at the beginning of the gospel on Sunday, the last judgment."],[744.63,"When the when the Son of Man comes with ten thousands of his angels."],[749.28,"Remember, St. Matthew's gospel, like I said, refers to the book of Eden."],[752.8,"Um, this is the day of judgment, is coming."],[757.14,"He's gonna execute judgment on all."],[759.78,"But these people St."],[761.58,"Judas talking about are ungodly."],[764.54,"They've done ungodly deeds in ungodly ways."],[769.15,"And the harsh things which they've spoken against God, the blasphemy against God, all these things that St."],[774.03,"Judas talking about."],[775.25,"That judgment is coming."],[779.86,"Verse 16, these are grumblers, complainers, walking according to their own lusts, and they mouth great swelling words, flattering people to gain advantage."],[790.26,"So you basically grumblers and complainers and then walking according to lust."],[794.99,"Those seem kind of like radically different categories."],[799.09,"Like they chew bubblegum and they fornicate."],[801.59,"Like one is not like the other."],[804.67,"But the specific words used there for grumblers and complainers are words drawn out of the Old Testament."],[811.33,"That's the word for the grumblers is the grumbling that Israel did in the murmuring, muttering, uh it's gungus me in in Greek."],[822.21,"Uh but um so it's not just a question of like, oh man, we gotta have mac and cheese again."],[831.19,"It's like this is talking about, you know, let's kill Moses and go back to Egypt, grumbling."],[838.88,"This is this is, you know, blasphemy, this is rejecting authority, plotting, you know, against the leadership of the church, trying to set themselves up as leaders."],[846.08,"Uh, like Korah"],[853.13,"Um there, but they're doing that for the purpose of satisfying their own lusts, not because they think they'd be good good Christian leaders."],[862.63,"And they're they go around flattering people to gain, they're playing politics."],[867.81,"Flattering people. But you, beloved, remember the words which were spoken before by the apostles of our Lord Jesus Christ."],[877.83,"So remember what the apostles told you when they came to you and gave you that gospel, gave you the faith originally."],[883.35,"You go back to that."],[884.39,"How they told you that there would be mockers in the last time, who would walk according to their own ungodly lusts."],[892.92,"So he says, you were warned that this was going to happen."],[896.92,"Remember the last time we talked about how this is referring to the Messianic Age."],[902.58,"That there are these sort of epochs, the last one starts when the Messiah comes and it ends on the last day."],[909.96,"When the judgment comes."],[911.16,"So that's where we're living now."],[913.48,"So you say, when the apostles came to you and announced that Jesus is the Messiah, announced that the Messianic Age had begun, brought you together as a community."],[921.28,"They told you there were going to be these mockers."],[924.08,"They were these people who are going to come and who are going to follow their own ungodly lusts."],[931.4,"Verse 19, these are sensual persons who cause divisions not having the Spirit."],[938.16,"So the Holy Spirit, in dwelling believers, brings them together, unites them together."],[944.77,"Forms them together into a community."],[947.33,"These people don't have the Holy Spirit."],[950.23,"They're against what God is doing in the community."],[953.27,"Draw people together, and so they cause divisions."],[955.11,"They split apart."],[956.95,"They divide so that they can then get power and control and satisfy their desires."],[965.91,"But you, beloved, building yourselves up on your most holy faith, praying in the Holy Spirit, keep yourselves in the love of God, looking for the mercy. of our Lord Jesus Christ unto eternal life"],[980.27,"So here's another Trinitarian passage."],[985.83,"So you don't do that."],[989.16,"You build yourself upon your most holy faith, that faith he mentioned, the faith the apostles brought you."],[993.72,"Not only do you need to fight to protect it, but you need to build your life on it."],[998.22,"You do that by praying in the Holy Spirit, which is what brings you together, unites you to each other and to God."],[1005.1,"Keep yourselves in the love of God."],[1007.6,"Which again, unity, that bond."],[1010.8,"Looking for the mercy of our Lord Jesus Christ, looking forward."],[1014.66,"Because remember, we just talked about the judgment that's coming for them."],[1018.02,"But you look for the mercy of Christ"],[1021.66,"That you'll receive his mercy at the time of that judgment."],[1024.06,"Because he's judging everybody."],[1026.24,"Look for his mercy unto eternal life when that time comes."],[1033.6,"Verse 22, and on some have compassion, making a distinction."],[1039.36,"But others save with fear, pulling them out of the fire, hating even the garment defiled by the by the flesh."],[1049.12,"Burn their clothes."],[1051.92,"Because it's contaminated."],[1053.84,"But notice what he doesn't say."],[1056.02,"He doesn't say, on some have compassion, making a distinction, and others let them burn."],[1062.92,"He's not saying judge."],[1064.92,"Here's what you need to do."],[1066.44,"You need to have an inquisition."],[1068.36,"You need to go and root out the heretics and destroy them."],[1072.06,"That's not what he says."],[1074.3,"He says, so there are some, out of this group, out of this group that's the problem."],[1080.67,"So you have this group of problem people in your community."],[1085.07,"You need to look at them."],[1086.35,"Some of them, don't know any better. are followers"],[1089.85,"Some of them are going along with this."],[1091.29,"Some of them have been led astray"],[1095.45,"You need to have compassion on those people."],[1098.49,"Steer them back."],[1099.79,"To the right path."],[1102.05,"But the ones who are the leaders, the ones who ones who know what they're doing, who are actively pursuing gratifying their own less, are starting."],[1110.87,"Who are the false teachers, who are trying to take power."],[1114.39,"Those people, again, you don't abandon."],[1117.79,"But you've got to pull them out of the fire."],[1120.29,"You're going to have to be a little more belligerent."],[1122.05,"You're going to have to be a little more aggressive."],[1124.42,"And we've talked about how Christ, St."],[1128.74,"Paul, the other apostles will use very harsh words toward people in certain circumstances."],[1134.76,"Think about Christ and the Pharisees."],[1137.32,"Calling them vipers, whitewashed tombs."],[1139.9,"Sons of the devil."],[1142.98,"And we made the point at that time. that Christ was still talking to them"],[1150.52,"He didn't just write them off and ignore them."],[1154.6,"He was still there talking to them."],[1156.36,"But he'd ramped up the rhetoric to try to get their attention."],[1161.42,"To try to snap them out of it."],[1164.22,"And that's what he's saying with these."],[1166.22,"These ones who are really far gone, who are there deliberately, who are motivating this."],[1172.0,"You're gonna have to deal with harshly."],[1173.92,"Don't deal with all of them harshly."],[1175.68,"Make this distinction."],[1176.56,"There's some who don't know any better."],[1178.8,"Be compassionate, be soft, be gentle with them."],[1182.56,"But the ones who are really motivating it, you're gonna have to be harsh with."],[1185.52,"But the goal is still to pull them out of the fire."],[1187.72,"What fire? That fire of judgment."],[1190.04,"It's coming to consume them on the last day if they don't repent."],[1195.32,"So to bring them to repentance, you're gonna have to be a little more harsh."],[1200.84,"You have to be a little more strong."],[1207.96,"That thing of hating even the garment defiled by the flesh."],[1211.32,"This is over against."],[1212.68,"Remember, they're a stain on your love feast at the Eucharist."],[1217.58,"You've been taking this approach of just like, hey, who am I to judge?"],[1222.06,"You know, let's just have peace."],[1225.51,"That's not how it works."],[1227.53,"You have peace and you have unity in the Holy Spirit, which means they need to repent and receive the Holy Spirit."],[1233.49,"Then you can add real unity together and real love for each other."],[1239.01,"But letting them come and receive the Eucharist and eat and drink condemnation on themselves without calling them to repentance is not love."],[1248.21,"That's not saving them."],[1251.01,"It's not warning them about the fire that's coming for them or pulling them out of it."],[1255.73,"That's what we need to do."],[1260.61,"Verse 24 Now to him who is able to keep you from stumbling and to present you faultless before the presence of his glory with exceeding joy."],[1271.11,"To God our Savior, who alone is wise, be glory and majesty, dominion and power, both now and forever."],[1277.95,"Amen. So notice not only do we have the praise of God, our Savior, who alone is wise, his glory, his majesty, his dominion, his power."],[1288.64,"But that same God who has all that dominion and power and who is coming to judge everyone is also the one who is able to keep you from stumbling and preserve you faultless and blameless before the presence of that glory when he comes to judge, with exceeding joy."],[1307.14,"So God is terrible in the original sense, but you don't have to be in terror of him."],[1315.88,"So there's awe and reverence for God who is coming to judge."],[1318.04,"ing. And we live our lives in repentance accord to that"],[1323.68,"But that repentance allows us to look toward that day with joy, with hope, for the mercy that's coming in Christ."],[1332.66,"And that's the end of St."],[1333.86,"Jude's Epistle. Let me ask you something."],[1336.34,"Yes, sir. I forgot."],[1342.02,"You've told us what it did."],[1345.86,"Yeah. So um so the New King James version is based on the same Greek text that the King James Version was based on."],[1359.78,"Uh, which is a sort of a a narrow slice of the text that we have and some of the later texts."],[1369.1,"And there are some folks who haven't studied this stuff a lot, who because those texts are referred to as Byzantine texts, assume that like those are the special magical orthodox text of the New Testament, which they're not."],[1390.32,"Um every Greek text of the New Testament that we have."],[1395.08,"Every single one was an Orthodox Bible."],[1402.08,"We found them at Orthodox monasteries."],[1404.65,"We found them at right."],[1406.77,"Um and so what's happened in the United States in particular, uh, because this all has to do with the English Bible and what texts are used to translate the English Bible."],[1422.63,"So, like the Russians don't have this problem."],[1425.19,"They got other problems, but they don't have this one."],[1429.33,"Um, because the Russian Synodal Bible was translated a long time ago from a whole different set of Greek texts, and there's no issue."],[1435.33,"Um but uh because the King James version was translated from a particular set of Greek texts, and because the King James Version sort of in its various revisions, it's been revised a number of times.es, um, in its various revisions was sort of the English Bible until the 20th century, for all intents and purpos"],[1457.21,"Yes, I know the Dewey Rehams was out there and stuff, but in America at least, King James Version was the Bible."],[1466.49,"Um we got what's now called King James onlyism in the Protestant world, uh, where people say like God inspired the particular English translation and the particular small set of texts was especially holy and all this."],[1484.09,"And some of that has come into the American Orthodox experience, even though it's foreign to it."],[1492.97,"Um again, the fact that the word Byzantine gets used in this context, people jump on."],[1502.53,"I've heard Orthodox people, even Orthodox priests, telling weird urban legends about certain Greek texts that aren't historically true at all."],[1512.37,"I'll give you an example."],[1514.53,"So one of the oldest Bibles we have is called Codex Sanaiticus."],[1519.49,"Okay. The New Testament is different in places from the text that the King James Version was based on."],[1529.01,"So Protestant King James only types hate it."],[1532.97,"Um and say all kinds of weird ahistorical things about it."],[1536.93,"So there are books written about how it's like a bunch of Jesuits forged. it"],[1542.09,"Um there's all kinds of weird, quasi conspiratorial things uh about it."],[1548.41,"Um oddly enough for Orthodox people who have picked up on those arguments about the New Test the New Testament of Codex Sinaticus, is that when you talk about the Greek Old Testament."],[1562.03,"Like if you go by the Greek Old Testament that this Orthodox Study Bible was translated from, Rolf's, is the Greek Old Testament from Codex Sinayaticus."],[1574.59,"So there are literally Orthodox folks out there who think the Old Testament of Codex Sinayaticus is the inspired version of the Old Testament, but its New Testament is like. the devil's lies"],[1588.31,"But so the biggest urban legend, though, they don't do usually do the Jesuit thing, Orthodox folks."],[1593.35,"At least I haven't run into them yet."],[1594.79,"There's probably somebody."],[1621.51,"Because it was full of errors."],[1623.91,"That's why they were going to burn it."],[1626.15,"So C, you can't trust it."],[1627.83,"Except for the Old Testament, which we think is canonical."],[1631.47,"Um, so here's the thing this is an urban legend, this is absolutely not true."],[1636.11,"Here's what actually happened."],[1637.95,"Tischendorf went and visited St."],[1639.87,"Catherine's modern series Mount Mass Sinai."],[1642.35,"One of the monks brought out to him this ancient manuscript of the Bible, wrapped in a communion cloth, wrapped in a kalima."],[1650.61,"Like the red cloth we use at communion that he was using to protect it."],[1655.01,"Okay. Gave it to Tishendorf."],[1657.17,"Tishendorf realized he didn't know how old, it's from the fourth century."],[1661.26,"Like shortly after St."],[1662.78,"Constantine. Um, but realized it was really old."],[1667.93,"And so he then went all over the monastery looking for because there were leaves that were missing."],[1676.15,"So with a codex like that, you had leaves that were copied and then they were stitched together into a into like a book."],[1684.17,"And so there were leaves missing."],[1686.26,"So he said, Well, I'm gonna go and see if I can find any of these missing leaves here in the monastery."],[1690.28,"He found a bunch in the library, found them in different places."],[1694.2,"He found some in the place where they burned old damaged texts and icons."],[1700.2,"Because if you're Orthodox, you should know when you have an old biblical text or an icon that's been damaged or worn, you dispose of it by burning it."],[1710.38,"And then you bury the ashes in a place where they're not gonna get stepped on."],[1713.58,"That's the that's the way to do that."],[1715.5,"So when they found oh, we've got these Bible pages that fell out of a Bible, the correct way to dispose of those loose pages is by burning."],[1724.34,"And he found some down there and salvaged them."],[1727.24,"He said, Oh no, I've got the book they came from."],[1729.26,"We can we can rebuild it."],[1730.54,"We have the technology."],[1731.7,"So he got it all together and then gave it to the czar as a gift."],[1738.18,"So he found it at St."],[1739.62,"Catharines, I'm outside of Orthodox Monster, and gave it to the czar."],[1743.86,"And it was in the Russian Imperial Library from the time he found it until uh Stalin started running out of money."],[1755.6,"So running out of money, and so he sold it to the Brits to get money to help bail out the Russian economy."],[1768.12,"That's how the Brits ended up with it."],[1771.96,"They got sold it by the Soviets."],[1774.76,"Um, this is one of like the most orthodox texts of the Bible ever vowed."],[1782.41,"Um, but the New Testament doesn't match the King James."],[1787.69,"So, yeah. Um, but yeah, so respectfully and unfortunately, some of the people who wrote the notes uh for the Orthodox City Bible New Testament kind of had some of that Protestant King James stuff floating around back there."],[1812.53,"And so you get some textual notes related to that."],[1817.73,"So the text that's up there being the New King James is based on that King James text."],[1822.05,"So they refer to the N U Tang."],[1823.81,"That's that other that other text."],[1827.89,"That's the Codex Aic."],[1829.49,"Codex Diac is another similar text."],[1831.17,"There's a couple more."],[1831.65,"There's one Codex Vaticanus, and uh there's a couple others from the fifth uh from the fourth century, fourth and fifth centuries."],[1840.05,"Um, what is it?"],[1846.53,"Uh I think it's Nubigen."],[1850.33,"Uh where they're putting together the So the Greek New Testament, if you go and buy a Greek New Testament, there's actually two different versions, but they're both being compiled uh in Germany."],[1864.01,"Um and they update it um based on new finds."],[1870.73,"They're cataloging everything, computerizing everything."],[1874.25,"Um and so that all gets put together into this one book you can buy in Greek that has like the uh the Greek text, and then down at the bottom it lists different variants."],[1890.25,"Like, oh these these manuscripts, these particular copies have this word instead of that word, or this letter instead of that letter down at the bottom."],[1901.06,"And so if you're of that kind of King James is the real Bible mystically mindset, then you're like, oh, you're just so in confusion about what the Bible says, you know, by listing all these things, and those things aren't relevant."],[1916.26,"And no, see, it's really cut and dried."],[1918.72,"Um you know, if you if you're gonna believe in sola scriptura, I kind of feel your pain."],[1924.56,"L,ike you gotta you gotta nail that down."],[1928.41,"If that's the way you're gonna go."],[1930.57,"But especially if you're not, if you're Orthodox, so you don't have sola scriptura anyway, I don't see any reason why. we shouldn't look at all the Orthodox Bibles"],[1940.45,"And not only does it have all those manuscripts down there in those footnotes, but if you read if you know how to read those footnotes, like it'll have church fathers who quote a slightly different version of the text."],[1952.13,"Like to me, that's kind of relevant."],[1955.59,"Like if my Orthodox study Bible has a verse a certain way and St."],[1959.03,"John Chrysostom quoted it in a slightly different way, I think that's good information for me to have as an Orthodox Christian."],[1967.37,"Or St. Gregory the Theologian or St."],[1969.05,"Basil. I don't know why, as an Orthodox Christian, like you wouldn't want that information."],[1975.1,"Why you'd be like, no, these 17th century Protestants, they nailed it."],[1980.38,"As opposed to the church fathers"],[1982.11,"Like. But uh, yeah."],[1988.43,"That's creeping around the edges of a lot of even American Orthodox stuff, especially when it comes to the Bible, even the Orthodox Study Bible."],[1996.99,"Um, and they'll you'll sometimes hear them say, This will be the last note I'll make on this, unless anybody asks any other questions specifically about it."],[2003.63,"They'll sometimes talk about that other text."],[2007.08,"Well, first of all, there's there's they'll they'll they'll talk about the majority text, the textus receptus, um uh there's another at least another one."],[2021.48,"Ecclesiastical text. There's a bunch of names, those actually aren't even the same text, technically."],[2025.0,"They talk of oh, the Byzantine text"],[2026.96,"So it gets really confusing."],[2030.96,"They'll talk about those like those are all the same thing, they're not the same thing."],[2035.04,"Um so there's even some weirdness there, and then they refer they refer to the other thing."],[2041.46,"The modern thing that compiles all the information from all the Orthodox Bibles as the eclectic text."],[2048.71,"Here's a problem with that."],[2050.23,"An eclectic text means it includes information from more than one manuscript, more than one copy."],[2057.29,"So the King James Version is based on an eclectic text."],[2060.73,"As soon as you have more than one copy."],[2063.34,"Erasmus, when he did his first printed Greek New Testament, only had six manuscripts."],[2067.98,"They couldn't find any more"],[2069.54,"He wanted more."],[2070.74,"The people who translated the King James Bible wanted more."],[2075.62,"If they could have found more, they would have used them."],[2078.1,"If they'd had Codex Side Anicus, they would have used it."],[2081.14,"They didn't have it."],[2081.95,"They didn't know it existed."],[2085.23,"But even if you only have six, like Erasmus, that's still eclectic, because you're taking more than one and you're putting them together."],[2092.03,"So everything is an eclectic text."],[2095.53,"So even though they talk about the eclectic text, like is this bad thing that the Jesuits andor the devil had a hand in somewhere?"],[2107.21,"It's not really uh a thing."],[2110.41,"When was the codex Sciaticus discovered 19th century that's a big time yeah late nineteenth century I don't have that to the year off the top of my head but if you go online they have scanned in the entire Codex Sinaiaticus."],[2127.25,"So if if you Google Codex Sciaticus they have a website I think it's Cyaticus.org you could page through the photos and look at the whole thing."],[2138.93,"No but it's kinda cool is it illuminate them?"],[2143.93,"No. No, it's un it's an unsealed manuscript."],[2148.13,"So at that time, most uh biblical manuscripts, New Testament manuscripts, written in all Greek capital letters."],[2156.85,"s Like no spaces between word."],[2157.61,"There's no spaces, there's no punctuation."],[2162.33,"And no punctuation."],[2163.61,"By that time, they were all going from left to right like this down the page."],[2168.49,"But before that, we have some earlier ones where they go like this."],[2177.69,"Yes, the easy small eyes."],[2185.29,"But yeah, it's uh Latin didn't have Posito."],[2189.69,"Yeah. Hieroglyphics, I think, can be read any direction."],[2195.45,"They can write."],[2196.33,"Yeah, yeah, they could write vertically."],[2198.41,"Vertically bottom to top, top to bottom, button, bottom."],[2210.37,"So. Does that overanswer your question?"],[2216.21,"Okay. Okay. Thank you, everybody."],[2223.41,"Thank you. Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2232.85,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2238.21,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.96,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 12-13 - Stains and empty clouds","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.12</b>: \"these are <b>spots</b> in your love feasts\" - feast with you <b>without fear</b>, serving only themselves</li><li class=\"d1\">\"spots\" = really <b>stains</b> - \"Lady Macbeth sense\"</li><li class=\"d1\">love feast = gathering to celebrate the <b>Eucharist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they're there, receiving like everybody else, <b>without a care in the world</b></li><li class=\"d1\">your <b>accepting them</b> and letting it go on = a <b>stain</b> on what you're doing, drags it down</li><li class=\"d0\">Images of useless</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>clouds without water</b> carried by winds - \"throws up some shade\", not even enough to stop sunburn</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>late autumn trees without fruit</b> - past harvest, useless</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>twice dead</b>, pulled up by the roots</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.13</b>: <b>raging waves</b> foaming up their own shame; <b>wandering stars</b> - <b>blackness of darkness forever</b> reserved</li><li class=\"d1\">\"bad people - he doesn't like them\"</li></ul>"},{"t":102.32,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 14-15 - Quoting Enoch (is it canon?)","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.14</b>: \"Now <b>Enoch, the seventh from Adam</b>, prophesied about these men also\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jude not only has <b>no problem</b> quoting the <b>book of Enoch</b> - quotes it <b>as Enoch</b>, seventh in the genealogy</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: so is he saying <b>1 Enoch</b> should be in the OT?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"If you're <b>Ethiopian</b>, sure\" - but <b>no</b>, he's not saying that</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul quoted <b>Menander</b> - great Greek comic playwright</li><li class=\"d1\">only one to win the <b>Athenian festival three times</b> - still not OT</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul could quote him to <b>Corinth</b> because it was someone they knew - \"even Paul made <b>pop culture references</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Jude has <b>no idea of canon</b></li><li class=\"d1\">quoting a text <b>all of them knew</b></li></ul>"},{"t":191.96,"lv":3,"h":"How popular Enoch was","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1 Enoch</b> = one of the <b>most popular Jewish texts</b> of the time</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b>: number of copies = how important a text was to a community</li><li class=\"d1\">copies of everything in our OT <b>except Esther and Ecclesiastes</b>, plus other stuff</li><li class=\"d1\">ranking: <b>#1 Genesis</b>, <b>#2 Enoch</b>, <b>#3 Jubilees</b>, <b>#4 Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in <b>Ethiopic OT</b>; Ethiopian Jews used it</li><li class=\"d1\">going around <b>Egypt</b> - copies found - \"all over the place\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So a story they'd <b>heard and knew</b></li></ul>"},{"t":256.67,"lv":2,"h":"Enoch in and out of fashion","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Later folks will have a problem with this - \"interesting to me anyway\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2nd - early 3rd c.</b>: Western fathers who know Jude (East mostly doesn't - not on their NT lists)</li><li class=\"d1\">argue Jude should be <b>read in churches</b>, be <b>canonical</b> - <b>because it quotes Enoch</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Enoch still very popular: <b>Irenaeus</b>, <b>Justin Martyr</b> all over it; <b>Tertullian</b> \"super into it\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>End of 3rd - 4th c.</b>: <b>opposite argument</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Enoch now \"on the outs\", Jude accepted by everyone</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Jude quotes Enoch, so you should accept Enoch\"</li></ul>"},{"t":368.6,"lv":3,"h":"Augustine and Enoch","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why Enoch went out of fashion: most of it falls out by the <b>5th century</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Augustine</b> \"pretty much hated\" Enoch</li><li class=\"d1\">Jude says Enoch wrote something - but that was <b>before the flood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how know our Enoch is the same? → \"you can't trust this\" - \"gymnastics\" to ignore it</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: why not the same logic on <b>Genesis</b>? - he liked Genesis; Moses wrote it <b>after the flood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Tertullian: whole stories that <b>Noah had a copy of Enoch on the ark</b> - \"don't know what the writing system would have been\"</li></ul>"},{"t":466.68,"lv":2,"h":"Enoch never really went away","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Common idea: fathers \"hashed it out and dumped it\" - <b>didn't happen</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>9th c.</b>: <b>St. Nikephoros</b>, patriarch of Constantinople - list of scriptural books from the library, categories</li><li class=\"d1\">books <b>all accept</b></li><li class=\"d1\">books <b>some communities accept</b></li><li class=\"d1\">books <b>not read in church but good to read at home</b> - <b>Enoch</b> here, with other Jewish texts</li><li class=\"d1\">Revelation still in the \"disagree\" list in the East</li><li class=\"d1\">copied in Orthodox monasteries up to <b>15th-16th c.</b> - then the <b>printing press</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>George Syncellus</b> with Nikephoros: <i>Byzantine Chronology</i></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>cribbed everything before the flood from Enoch</b> - treated as historical document</li><li class=\"d1\">\"always been kind of <b>Bible adjacent</b> in the East\" - people just don't know</li></ul>"},{"t":602.84,"lv":3,"h":"The Enoch quote in Jude","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.14-15</b>: \"Behold, the Lord comes with <b>ten thousands of his saints</b> to execute judgment on all...\" - <b>direct quote</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Augustine: fine, that one verse - but Enoch has <b>100+ chapters</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Enoch also a <b>composite book</b> - chunks that circulated separately, different vocabulary, different visions</li><li class=\"d0\">Will come up again: references in <b>1 Peter</b>, <b>2 Peter</b>, <b>Revelation</b> (full of them), <b>Matthew's gospel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">read by writers and readers of NT, early church, fathers</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not in formal worship</b> for most - read <b>at home</b></li><li class=\"d1\">exceptions: <b>Qumran</b>, <b>Ethiopia</b></li></ul>"},{"t":726.12,"lv":3,"h":"Enoch's judgment fits Jude's theme","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ten thousands of his holy ones</b> = <b>angels</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>last judgment</b> - Sunday gospel: Son of Man comes with ten thousands of angels</li><li class=\"d1\">Matthew refers to Enoch</li><li class=\"d0\">Day of judgment coming - <b>execute judgment on all</b></li><li class=\"d1\">these people: <b>ungodly</b> deeds, ungodly ways, <b>harsh words against God</b> - blasphemy</li></ul>"},{"t":779.86,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 16 - Grumblers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.16</b>: <b>grumblers, complainers</b>, walking by their own <b>lusts</b>, <b>swelling words</b>, <b>flattering</b> for advantage</li><li class=\"d1\">grumbling + lust seem like different categories - \"chew bubblegum and fornicate\"</li><li class=\"d1\">words come from the <b>OT</b>: <b>murmuring</b> of Israel (<i>gongusmos</i>)</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"we gotta have mac and cheese again\" - <b>\"kill Moses and go back to Egypt\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>blasphemy</b>, rejecting authority, <b>plotting against church leadership</b>, setting themselves up - like <b>Korah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">done to <b>satisfy own lusts</b>, not because they'd be good leaders</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>playing politics</b> - flattering people</li></ul>"},{"t":867.81,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 17-19 - Remember the apostles' warning","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.17</b>: \"remember the words spoken before by the <b>apostles</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">when they gave you the gospel, the faith - <b>go back to that</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.18</b>: <b>mockers in the last time</b>, walking by <b>ungodly lusts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">you were <b>warned</b> this would happen</li><li class=\"d1\">\"last time\" = the <b>Messianic Age</b> - epochs; last starts when <b>Messiah comes</b>, ends on <b>last day</b> - where we live now</li><li class=\"d1\">apostles announced Jesus is Messiah, age begun, brought you together</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.19</b>: <b>sensual persons</b> causing divisions, <b>not having the Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Spirit in believers <b>unites</b>, forms a community</li><li class=\"d1\">these <b>don't have the Spirit</b> - against what God is doing</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>split apart</b> to get <b>power and control</b>, satisfy desires</li></ul>"},{"t":965.91,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 20-21 - Build yourselves up","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.20-21</b>: \"<b>building yourselves up</b> on your most holy faith, <b>praying in the Holy Spirit</b>, keep yourselves in the <b>love of God</b>, looking for the <b>mercy</b> of our Lord Jesus Christ unto eternal life\"</li><li class=\"d1\">another <b>Trinitarian</b> passage</li><li class=\"d1\">build on <b>faith the apostles brought</b> - not only fight to protect it, <b>build your life on it</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>praying in the Spirit</b> - unites you to each other and God</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>love of God</b> - again unity, <b>bond</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>looking for mercy</b> - judgment coming for them, but you look for Christ's mercy at the time of judgment - he judges everybody</li><li class=\"d1\">mercy <b>unto eternal life</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1033.6,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 22-23 - Compassion, and pulling from the fire","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.22-23</b>: <b>some have compassion</b>, making a distinction; others <b>save with fear, pulling out of the fire</b>, <b>hating even the garment defiled by the flesh</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"burn their clothes - contaminated\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Notice what he <b>doesn't</b> say</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"others let them burn\"; not \"have an <b>inquisition</b>, root out and destroy heretics\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Two groups out of the problem group</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>followers</b>, don't know better, led astray → <b>compassion</b>, steer back</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>leaders</b>, know what they're doing, <b>false teachers</b> seeking power → don't abandon, but <b>pull out of the fire</b> - more <b>belligerent, aggressive</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Christ and the Pharisees</b>: vipers, whitewashed tombs, sons of the devil</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ was <b>still talking to them</b>, ramped up rhetoric to <b>snap them out of it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">harsh only with the ones <b>motivating</b> it; soft with the rest</li><li class=\"d1\">goal still <b>pull them out</b> - fire of <b>judgment</b> on the last day, if no repentance</li></ul>"},{"t":1207.96,"lv":3,"h":"Not love: letting them stay","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hating the garment</b> - against being a <b>stain on your love feast</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"hey, who am I to judge? let's just have peace\" - <b>not how it works</b></li><li class=\"d1\">peace and unity <b>in the Holy Spirit</b> = they must <b>repent and receive the Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then <b>real unity</b> and love</li><li class=\"d0\">Letting them come to the <b>Eucharist</b> and <b>eat and drink condemnation</b> without calling to repentance = <b>not love</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not saving them, not warning about the fire, not pulling out</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's what we need to do\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1260.61,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 24-25 - Doxology","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.24-25</b>: to him <b>able to keep you from stumbling</b>, present you <b>faultless</b> before his glory with <b>exceeding joy</b> - to <b>God our Savior, who alone is wise</b>, glory, majesty, dominion, power, now and forever</li><li class=\"d1\">praise of God: glory, majesty, dominion, power</li><li class=\"d1\">same God who is coming to <b>judge everyone</b> is the one who <b>keeps you from stumbling</b>, preserves you <b>blameless</b> before that glory with <b>exceeding joy</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"God is terrible in the original sense\"</b> - but no need to be <b>in terror</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>awe and reverence</b> for the judge</li><li class=\"d1\">live in <b>repentance</b> → look to that day with <b>joy, hope</b>, for the <b>mercy</b> coming in Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">End of <b>St. Jude's Epistle</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1333.86,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: NKJV, Byzantine text, King James","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: (about the textual correction in <b>v.5</b>) the NKJV text</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>NKJV</b> based on same Greek text as <b>King James</b> - \"narrow slice\" of the texts, some later ones</li><li class=\"d0\">Folks assume <b>\"Byzantine\"</b> texts = \"the special magical orthodox text\" - <b>they're not</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>every</b> Greek NT manuscript was an Orthodox Bible - found at <b>Orthodox monasteries</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Russians don't have this problem - <b>Synodal Bible</b> from a whole different set of Greek texts</li></ul>"},{"t":1435.33,"lv":3,"h":"King James onlyism in Orthodoxy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">KJV = <b>the English Bible</b> until the 20th c. (Douay-Rheims aside) - in America <b>the Bible</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hence <b>King James onlyism</b> in Protestant world: God inspired <b>that English translation</b>, that small set of texts</li><li class=\"d1\">some has come into <b>American Orthodox</b> experience - foreign to it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Byzantine\" label makes people jump on it</li><li class=\"d1\">Orthodox priests telling <b>urban legends</b> about Greek texts - not historically true</li></ul>"},{"t":1514.53,"lv":2,"h":"Codex Sinaiticus urban legend","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Codex Sinaiticus</b> - one of the <b>oldest Bibles</b>; NT differs from the text behind KJV</li><li class=\"d1\">KJV-only types hate it, say weird ahistorical things - books say <b>Jesuits forged it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">irony: the <b>Greek OT</b> behind the <b>Orthodox Study Bible</b> (Rahlfs) is from <b>Sinaiticus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Orthodox folks who think its <b>OT inspired</b> but its <b>NT \"the devil's lies\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">The big Orthodox urban legend (transcript gap here): monks were going to <b>burn</b> it because <b>full of errors</b> → \"can't trust it, except the OT\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"absolutely not true\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1631.47,"lv":3,"h":"What actually happened to Sinaiticus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Tischendorf</b> visited <b>St. Catherine's</b>, Mt. Sinai</li><li class=\"d1\">a monk brought out an ancient Bible <b>wrapped in a communion cloth</b> (like the red cloth at communion)</li><li class=\"d1\">Tischendorf realized it was old - <b>4th century</b>, shortly after Constantine</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>leaves missing</b> - codex = leaves stitched together - searched the monastery</li><li class=\"d1\">found some in the library, some where they <b>burn old damaged texts and icons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Orthodox disposal: <b>burn</b>, bury ashes where not stepped on</li><li class=\"d1\">salvaged them: \"we have the technology\"</li><li class=\"d1\">gave it to the <b>czar</b> as a gift</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Russian Imperial Library</b> until <b>Stalin</b> ran out of money → sold to the <b>Brits</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"one of the most <b>orthodox</b> texts of the Bible ever\" - but NT doesn't match KJV</li></ul>"},{"t":1787.69,"lv":2,"h":"Orthodox Study Bible notes and the critical text","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Some who wrote the <b>Orthodox Study Bible NT</b> notes had <b>Protestant King James stuff</b> floating around - textual notes reflect it</li><li class=\"d1\">text up there = <b>NKJV</b>, based on KJV text</li><li class=\"d1\">notes refer to <b>NU text</b> (Nestle-Aland / United Bible Societies)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Codex Alexandrinus, Vaticanus</b> etc. - <b>4th-5th c.</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Greek NT</b> you can buy - two versions, compiled in <b>Germany</b></li><li class=\"d1\">updated with new finds, cataloging, computerizing</li><li class=\"d1\">text on top, <b>variants at the bottom</b>: these copies have this word, that letter</li><li class=\"d0\">KJV-mystics: \"you're just confusing\" - \"if you're going to believe in <b>sola scriptura</b>, I kind of feel your pain\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1930.57,"lv":3,"h":"Why the footnotes matter to Orthodox","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox don't have sola scriptura - <b>no reason not to look at all the Orthodox Bibles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">footnotes list manuscripts, plus <b>church fathers</b> who quote a slightly different version</li><li class=\"d1\">if <b>Chrysostom</b>, <b>Gregory the Theologian</b>, <b>Basil</b> quote it differently - \"good information for me as an Orthodox Christian\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"17th century Protestants nailed it - <b>as opposed to the church fathers</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Creeping around the edges of American Orthodoxy, even the <b>Orthodox Study Bible</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2007.08,"lv":2,"h":"\"Eclectic text\" is a non-problem","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Names: <b>majority text</b>, <b>Textus Receptus</b>, <b>ecclesiastical text</b>, <b>Byzantine text</b> - not the same texts technically; confusing</li><li class=\"d1\">the modern text compiled from all the manuscripts called <b>eclectic</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Eclectic</b> = from <b>more than one manuscript</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>KJV</b> is eclectic too - <b>Erasmus</b> had only <b>six manuscripts</b> - \"if they could have found more, they would have used them\"</li><li class=\"d1\">KJV translators would have used Sinaiticus - didn't know it existed</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>everything is an eclectic text</b> - not a bad thing the <b>Jesuits and/or the devil</b> had a hand in</li></ul>"},{"t":2110.41,"lv":3,"h":"Q: When found; the manuscript itself","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: when was <b>Sinaiticus</b> discovered? - <b>late 19th c.</b> - <b>entire codex scanned online</b>: Google it, website lets you <b>page through the photos</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: illuminated? - no, <b>unsealed</b>/plain manuscript</li><li class=\"d1\">Greek NT manuscripts then: <b>all capital letters</b>, <b>no spaces</b>, <b>no punctuation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">by then going <b>left to right</b> down the page; <b>earlier ones</b> go differently (boustrophedon-style)</li><li class=\"d1\">hieroglyphics readable <b>any direction</b>, vertical too</li><li class=\"d0\">Closing: \"does that overanswer your question?\"</li></ul>"}]});
